The NVIDIA toolchain requires us to make `CXX` and `CC` point to the C++
compiler, as both are passed the same arguments by configure, which are
actually only understood by the C++ version.


# NVIDIA analysis tools 

Peano offers a dedicated @ref tarch_logging "NVIDIA logger".
It pipes tracing information into a format that NSight et al can process, i.e. it provides you with domain-specific information on top of your profile.
To enable NVIDIA's NVPTX logging, you have to configure with the

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
--with-toolchain=nvptx
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

toolchain.


# OpenMP

NVIDIA's compiler does not support all of OpenMP. Therefore, its OpenMP
implementation is rather picky when it comes to taskloops and
other C++ features. Peano has internal workarounds for all of these items,
i.e. the build should in principle succeed yet the code might be
slightly slower than the Intel or native Clang counterpart.


To be compatible with NVIDIA, the respective tricky code parts all are 
embedded into proper ifdefs. The idea is that every file includes the 
compiler-specific settings:


\include tarch/compiler/CompilerSpecificSettings.h

The header file reads out preprocessors and delegates to the right header
file. Within the header file, we have a lot of defines. Below is a copy of
the header file which should be used for NVIDIA on Linux systems.

\include tarch/compiler/LinuxNVIDIA.h

You see for example that we explicitly undefine OpenMPTaskGroup, as task 
groups is one of the things NVIDIA does not support at the moment. So the 
corresponding code parts employing task groups can read out this ifdef and
then provide fitting alternatives.

## OpenMP offloading to GPUs

The OpenMP offloading requires us to specify both the GPU OpenMP target
and a corresponding compute capability:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
CC=nvc++ CXX=nvc++ ./configure --with-multithreading=omp --with-gpu=omp CFLAGS="-O3 -W -Wall --diag_suppress=unrecognized_attribute -fopenmp -Munroll=c:100 -mp=gpu -gpu=ccXX" CXX="nvc++" CXXFLAGS="-std=c++20 -O3 -W -Wall --diag_suppress=unrecognized_attribute -fopenmp -Munroll=c:100 -mp=gpu -gpu=ccXX" LDFLAGS="-march=native -mtune=native -fopenmp -Munroll=c:100 -mp=gpu -gpu=ccXX"
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

where XX has to be replaced by the actual compute capability of the device.


# C++

We have experimental support for std::par offloading. To enable it with NVIDIA, you have to specify

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
CC=nvc++ CXX=nvc++ CFLAGS="-O3 -W -Wall --diag_suppress=unrecognized_attribute -fopenmp -Munroll=c:100 -mp=gpu -gpu=ccXX" CC="nvc" CXXFLAGS="-std=c++20 -O3 -W -Wall --diag_suppress=unrecognized_attribute -fopenmp -Munroll=c:100 -mp=gpu -stdpar=gpu -gpu=ccXX" LDFLAGS="-march=native -mtune=native -fopenmp -Munroll=c:100 -mp=gpu -gpu=ccXX"
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

where XX has to be replaced by the actual compute capability of the device. 
Note that the above setup still uses OpenMP on the host for the multithreading. 



